@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as text with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is the JSON re-serialized over multiple lines, indented by four spaces by default, with syntax highlighting. Pass ``indent`` to change the number of spaces, or ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same output is available as a renderable, :class:`~rich.json.JSON`, which you can pass to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, but default printing still crops lines that are wider than the console. To keep a long line whole, enable soft wrapping with ``soft_wrap=True``, as the command-line entry point does.
+
+There is also a package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich"}')
+
+The json module can also be run as a program to pretty print a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
